ext4 create: accept a caller-supplied final inode mode

The upstream create paths hard-code their default permission bits. That makes
POSIX open(O_CREAT, mode), mkdir(mode), and umask impossible to implement at
the shared filesystem boundary without a racy create-then-chmod sequence.

Add mode-aware entry points while keeping the existing APIs as exact wrappers
for the upstream default. The requested low twelve bits are written before the
new inode is linked, so another observer can never see the wrong permissions.

Covered by sw/userspace/storage's `make ext4-test`, which creates a 0600 file
and a 0710 directory and reads both inode modes back from raw and partitioned
volumes.

